  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nCurrently the afternoon convection has started in the far southern \nAppalachian Mountains on the TN/NC/GA border area. There is very \nlittle steering flow present out there as ridging builds in from the \nwest.\n\nThe upcoming week looks to be a rather unpleasant return to hot and \nmuggy conditions as an upper level ridge locks in over the southern \nUnited States. This will lead to temperatures climbing into the 90's \nfor most locations every afternoon this week. And unfortunately \nalong with this the dew points will rise back into the upper 60's to \nlower 70's making for high heat index values near or just above the \ntriple digit mark. Any afternoon convection that does fire should \nform along the Smokies and drift east into the Carolinas. Outside of \nthe southern Appalachian Mountains it will remain pretty dry this \nweek for most of the Valley. There is a possibility a shortwave \nmoving around the ridge comes in a bit stronger and is able to make \na dent into it and bring in more widespread storms, but this looks \nsomewhat unlikely at this time. Looking ahead, there is some signal \nto support a stronger trough just in time for next weekend. That \ncould help nudge this ridge back to the west and allow for slightly \ncooler and drier air to return.",
